  ┌─ Namespace isolation: PID 1 vs self
  [0;35m[NOTE][0m  Each namespace type in Linux isolates a resource class.
  [0;35m[NOTE][0m    mnt = filesystems   net = network stack   pid = process IDs
  [0;35m[NOTE][0m    ipc = shared memory   uts = hostname   user = uid/gid mapping
  [0;35m[NOTE][0m  If this process shares a namespace inode with PID 1, that resource is NOT isolated.

    $ readlink /proc/1/ns/*
    /proc/1/ns/mnt    → mnt:[4026532177]
    /proc/1/ns/net    → net:[4026531840]
    /proc/1/ns/pid    → pid:[4026532176]
    /proc/1/ns/ipc    → ipc:[4026532146]
    /proc/1/ns/uts    → uts:[4026532147]
    /proc/1/ns/user   → user:[4026531837]

    $ readlink /proc/self/ns/*
    /proc/self/ns/mnt    → mnt:[4026532177]
    /proc/self/ns/net    → net:[4026531840]
    /proc/self/ns/pid    → pid:[4026532176]
    /proc/self/ns/ipc    → ipc:[4026532146]
    /proc/self/ns/uts    → uts:[4026532147]
    /proc/self/ns/user   → user:[4026531837]

